WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.866
This briefing comes from Book 17 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.866 --> 00:00:08.238
AI Identity Governance, the
last book in the series.

3
00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:12.184
It's written for Chief AI
Officers, C I Os and CISOs,

4
00:00:12.184 --> 00:00:18.300
identity and security operations teams,
I S S Os, and authorizing officials.

5
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.679
I'll take four questions: why AI
agents need identities and owners;

6
00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:26.085
what governing them involves;
who owns AI identity;

7
00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:30.950
and how to scan the inventory and
evidence AI systems for authorization.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.500
The short version: an AI system that
can act is an account with permissions.

9
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.498
Most were deployed outside
the identity program,

10
00:00:38.498 --> 00:00:42.230
and there's no separate AI
authorization to fall back on.

11
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.880
A chat assistant that
drafts a memo is a tool.

12
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.305
Nothing it does reaches a record until
a person reads the draft and acts.

13
00:00:50.305 --> 00:00:51.743
An agent is different.

14
00:00:51.743 --> 00:00:56.692
It sends replies from a mailbox, closes
tickets and updates case records.

15
00:00:56.692 --> 00:00:59.227
Each step is an action
against agency data,

16
00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:02.630
taken by something that signed
in and holds permissions.

17
00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.568
That makes an agent an identity.

18
00:01:04.568 --> 00:01:09.876
An AI system that acts reaches data
in one of four ways: on behalf of a

19
00:01:09.876 --> 00:01:14.992
signed-in user, as itself, as its own
user account, or with an A P I key.

20
00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:18.042
The first three run through
the identity provider.

21
00:01:18.042 --> 00:01:20.295
The fourth bypasses it entirely,

22
00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:24.717
so Conditional Access never applies
and no sign-in is evaluated.

23
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.981
Today the agentic share is small.

24
00:01:26.981 --> 00:01:35.305
OMB's 2025 inventory holds 3,611
use cases, 445 of them high-impact.

25
00:01:35.305 --> 00:01:40.792
117 are classified agentic, and 38
of those are in pilot or deployed.

26
00:01:40.792 --> 00:01:43.570
It's also the share that grows fastest.

27
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.088
People join through a pipeline
the identity program built,

28
00:01:48.088 --> 00:01:50.395
from an HR record to a leaver process.

29
00:01:50.395 --> 00:01:52.245
AI mostly came in another way.

30
00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.758
A mission office ran
a pilot with a vendor.

31
00:01:54.758 --> 00:01:58.683
A developer put an A P I
key in a configuration file.

32
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:02.457
A product the agency already
licensed shipped an assistant.

33
00:02:02.457 --> 00:02:05.531
None of those paths produces
an owner of record,

34
00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:08.683
a placement, a governed
credential or an end date.

35
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:10.683
The risk is identity-shaped.

36
00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:14.520
NIST's generative AI profile
describes prompt injection,

37
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.558
where planted input
makes a system misbehave.

38
00:02:17.558 --> 00:02:22.413
Against an agent with broad permissions,
a successful injection is an action,

39
00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:26.358
a message sent or a record changed,
under a legitimate identity.

40
00:02:26.358 --> 00:02:29.232
The blast radius is the access it holds.

41
00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.257
No new control family is needed.

42
00:02:31.257 --> 00:02:34.890
Account management, identifier
and authenticator management,

43
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:39.482
least privilege and service authentication
already cover non-person accounts.

44
00:02:39.482 --> 00:02:43.145
The gap is coverage: the AI
system's account is often missing

45
00:02:43.145 --> 00:02:47.230
from the account inventory the
controls are tested against.

46
00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.393
Federal AI policy was rewritten in 2025.

47
00:02:51.393 --> 00:02:56.873
OMB memo M twenty-five twenty-one governs
use: a Chief AI Officer, an annual

48
00:02:56.873 --> 00:02:59.704
use-case inventory, compliance plans,

49
00:02:59.704 --> 00:03:03.267
and seven minimum practices
for high-impact AI.

50
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:08.395
Its companion, M twenty-five twenty-two,
governs acquisition, and says AI operated

51
00:03:08.395 --> 00:03:13.905
as an information system must receive an
authorization to operate before deployment.

52
00:03:13.905 --> 00:03:16.180
What the rules don't do matters as much.

53
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.718
There is no AI-specific authorization.

54
00:03:18.718 --> 00:03:23.886
Agencies must keep following Circular
A-130 for AI systems, and the memo's own

55
00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:29.218
risk acceptance is separate from, and
doesn't supersede, system authorization.

56
00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:31.643
It sets no identity requirements at all.

57
00:03:31.643 --> 00:03:35.372
So a high-impact use case needs
two decisions before it deploys:

58
00:03:35.372 --> 00:03:40.392
the signed risk acceptance on its impact
assessment, and the system's authorization.

59
00:03:40.392 --> 00:03:41.417
One more note.

60
00:03:41.417 --> 00:03:45.183
Executive Order fourteen four
thirty-four tells agencies

61
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:48.642
to say Super Intelligence
in non-statutory documents.

62
00:03:48.642 --> 00:03:52.289
The definition hasn't changed,
and the book keeps the term AI,

63
00:03:52.289 --> 00:03:55.620
because the instruments
it applies still use it.

64
00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.444
The inventory is the only
government-wide list of federal AI,

65
00:04:00.444 --> 00:04:03.133
so the program reads it as identity data.

66
00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:04.795
A few fields are anchors.

67
00:04:04.795 --> 00:04:09.270
The use-case ID is stable and never
reused, so it can key a record.

68
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.933
The bureau gives a placement.

69
00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:15.333
The stage is a lifecycle state,
and Retired is a leaver event.

70
00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:19.286
Other fields are leads to check:
the contact may be a public inbox,

71
00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:22.345
and the authorization
field points to a boundary.

72
00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:26.883
The facts that decide access aren't
there at all: the principal the system

73
00:04:26.883 --> 00:04:31.345
signs in as, its credential, its
permissions, and the person accountable.

74
00:04:31.345 --> 00:04:34.707
The high-impact fields
report status, not evidence.

75
00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:39.758
Each of the seven practices has a field,
but each holds the agency's own answer.

76
00:04:39.758 --> 00:04:41.458
In the published file,

77
00:04:41.458 --> 00:04:46.370
101 of 227 deployed high-impact
use cases answered none of them.

78
00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.363
And a use case isn't an identity,

79
00:04:48.363 --> 00:04:52.230
so the program records the
join on the directory side.

80
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.962
An AI system's kind of
identity is decided by

81
00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:59.193
whose token it presents,
not by the model it runs.

82
00:04:59.193 --> 00:05:00.606
There are four answers.

83
00:05:00.606 --> 00:05:05.621
Acting for a signed-in user, the user is
the token's subject and the agent is the

84
00:05:05.621 --> 00:05:07.850
actor, so the user's policies apply,

85
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.955
and the agent can reach
everything the user can.

86
00:05:10.955 --> 00:05:14.740
Acting as itself, the agent's
own identity is the subject,

87
00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.442
and the only Conditional
Access control is to block it.

88
00:05:18.442 --> 00:05:23.517
Acting as its own user account, it isn't
covered by policies aimed at all users.

89
00:05:23.517 --> 00:05:28.304
And with a stored A P I key,
Entra ID issues no token at all.

90
00:05:28.304 --> 00:05:33.598
Microsoft Entra Agent ID adds identity
types built for agents: a blueprint that

91
00:05:33.598 --> 00:05:37.368
holds the credentials, agent
identities created from it,

92
00:05:37.368 --> 00:05:39.293
and a required human sponsor.

93
00:05:39.293 --> 00:05:41.692
It governs only agents that use it.

94
00:05:41.692 --> 00:05:46.261
Most AI still runs as ordinary service
principals and managed identities,

95
00:05:46.261 --> 00:05:49.050
and the same governance applies to them.

96
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.708
The agency's cloud
decides what it can use,

97
00:05:52.708 --> 00:05:56.813
so the book dates every product
statement to October 2026.

98
00:05:56.813 --> 00:06:02.537
Microsoft announced the Agent ID platform,
with blueprints, agent identities and

99
00:06:02.537 --> 00:06:05.821
sponsors, as generally
available in April 2026,

100
00:06:05.821 --> 00:06:08.599
and sponsor lifecycle workflows in May.

101
00:06:08.599 --> 00:06:10.775
Several controls are still preview.

102
00:06:10.775 --> 00:06:15.677
The policy builder labels agent targeting
in Conditional Access as preview,

103
00:06:15.677 --> 00:06:20.349
agent identities support only Block,
the agent risk condition is preview,

104
00:06:20.349 --> 00:06:24.562
and agents' user accounts are
limited to Frontier program tenants.

105
00:06:24.562 --> 00:06:30.175
GCC has a partial, documented subset:
identity and access governance for agents,

106
00:06:30.175 --> 00:06:34.937
but not Microsoft's audit, data loss
prevention or network controls.

107
00:06:34.937 --> 00:06:38.223
GCC High and D O D have no
published availability,

108
00:06:38.223 --> 00:06:42.657
so treat agent identities as
unavailable there until Microsoft

109
00:06:42.657 --> 00:06:47.338
confirms otherwise in writing, and
govern AI as workload identities.

110
00:06:47.338 --> 00:06:52.025
And Agent 365's FedRAMP High
authorization is still pending.

111
00:06:52.025 --> 00:06:56.499
The program builds on what is generally
available in the agency's own cloud,

112
00:06:56.499 --> 00:06:59.390
and keeps preview features as watch items.

113
00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:03.236
An agent has no hire date
and no separation date,

114
00:07:03.236 --> 00:07:06.490
so its lifecycle has to hang
from a person who does.

115
00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:11.418
Agent ID separates the owner, who manages
credentials, from the sponsor, who is

116
00:07:11.418 --> 00:07:16.578
accountable for purpose and lifecycle and
can switch an agent off but not back on.

117
00:07:16.578 --> 00:07:20.153
Left to defaults, the sponsor
is whoever built the agent.

118
00:07:20.153 --> 00:07:25.028
The program decides instead: the sponsor
is a federal employee mastered in HR,

119
00:07:25.028 --> 00:07:28.533
a small dynamic group of
stewards is the second sponsor,

120
00:07:28.533 --> 00:07:31.352
and the technical owner
is never the sponsor.

121
00:07:31.352 --> 00:07:33.365
The lifecycle follows that person.

122
00:07:33.365 --> 00:07:37.432
When the sponsor moves or leaves,
the HR event triggers a transfer,

123
00:07:37.432 --> 00:07:42.252
and the new sponsor has ten business days
to confirm the agent is still needed.

124
00:07:42.252 --> 00:07:45.308
Access comes through
packages with end dates,

125
00:07:45.308 --> 00:07:49.891
so expiry is the review: a sponsor
who does nothing removes the access.

126
00:07:49.891 --> 00:07:52.890
Retirement is disable first, then delete.

127
00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:58.341
Once an orphaned agent is confirmed, a
person disables it, never an automatic rule,

128
00:07:58.341 --> 00:08:02.690
and its office has thirty days
to name a sponsor or retire it.

129
00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.170
An agent can't be asked
for a second factor,

130
00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:09.140
so everything it may do
is decided in advance.

131
00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:10.578
Start with the credential.

132
00:08:10.578 --> 00:08:15.696
In Agent ID it lives on the blueprint and
is shared by every agent created from it,

133
00:08:15.696 --> 00:08:18.565
so the blueprint is the
credential boundary.

134
00:08:18.565 --> 00:08:23.466
The order is settled: a managed identity
federated to the blueprint where the agent

135
00:08:23.466 --> 00:08:28.011
runs on Azure, workload identity federation
from its own platform elsewhere,

136
00:08:28.011 --> 00:08:31.065
a certificate in a vault
only where neither applies,

137
00:08:31.065 --> 00:08:33.977
and a client secret only
for development and test.

138
00:08:33.977 --> 00:08:35.152
Then privilege.

139
00:08:35.152 --> 00:08:38.314
Microsoft blocks agents
from Global Administrator,

140
00:08:38.314 --> 00:08:41.764
but still allows Exchange
and SharePoint Administrator.

141
00:08:41.764 --> 00:08:43.969
That's a floor, not least privilege,

142
00:08:43.969 --> 00:08:48.927
so the program's rule is that no AI
identity holds a standing privileged role.

143
00:08:48.927 --> 00:08:52.152
Last, an agent's reach is
bounded by its tokens,

144
00:08:52.152 --> 00:08:55.028
its approved tools, and data protection.

145
00:08:55.028 --> 00:08:58.320
In GCC, Purview's agent
controls aren't available,

146
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.720
so scoped permissions
are the data boundary.

147
00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:07.098
Every AI identity drifts once it
exists: a sponsor changes jobs,

148
00:09:07.098 --> 00:09:10.658
a developer adds a secret,
an agent gets a new tool.

149
00:09:10.658 --> 00:09:15.657
M twenty-five twenty-one asks monitoring
of high-impact AI to detect changes to a

150
00:09:15.657 --> 00:09:20.808
system after deployment, and the identity
logs are where those changes land first.

151
00:09:20.808 --> 00:09:26.190
So the program collects every AI identity's
sign-ins, audit events and risk signals,

152
00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.803
and ties each one to its identity record,

153
00:09:28.803 --> 00:09:32.682
because nothing in a service
principal's sign-in says it's AI.

154
00:09:32.682 --> 00:09:34.845
Discovery is a standing input.

155
00:09:34.845 --> 00:09:39.850
Shadow AI shows up in four places:
services people use, apps holding

156
00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:45.583
consented access, agents on endpoints,
and AI built in the agency's own clouds.

157
00:09:45.583 --> 00:09:48.508
Anything found without
a record is a finding.

158
00:09:48.508 --> 00:09:51.794
An agentic AI system with
no governed identity

159
00:09:51.794 --> 00:09:54.744
is the finding this
book exists to prevent.

160
00:09:54.744 --> 00:09:58.201
It's high priority: it halts,
alerts a named person,

161
00:09:58.201 --> 00:10:00.620
and is never fixed automatically.

162
00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.040
And A P I keys stay
invisible to all of it.

163
00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:09.390
Ask who owns an AI agent's identity
and three offices answer at once.

164
00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:13.778
The way out is to separate the decisions
and give each one a single decider.

165
00:10:13.778 --> 00:10:17.427
AI adds one new executive
and one new kind of decision.

166
00:10:17.427 --> 00:10:19.854
The Chief AI Officer owns the inventory,

167
00:10:19.854 --> 00:10:24.778
high-impact determinations and waivers,
and can't delegate the waiver authority.

168
00:10:24.778 --> 00:10:29.128
The official accountable for the mission
signs the use-case risk acceptance.

169
00:10:29.128 --> 00:10:31.027
Neither authorizes the system.

170
00:10:31.027 --> 00:10:36.153
The authorizing official alone accepts
its residual security and privacy risk,

171
00:10:36.153 --> 00:10:41.278
and the system owner owns the AI system
and decides what its identities may reach.

172
00:10:41.278 --> 00:10:44.859
For the identity itself, the
sponsor, an agency employee,

173
00:10:44.859 --> 00:10:48.752
decides whether the agent should
exist; the technical owner,

174
00:10:48.752 --> 00:10:51.321
who may be a contractor, keeps it running;

175
00:10:51.321 --> 00:10:56.303
and the identity governance officer
decides consent, transfers and orphans.

176
00:10:56.303 --> 00:11:00.960
Two signatures stay two, even
when one person holds both roles.

177
00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:05.900
The inventory scan is the program's
method, not a federal requirement,

178
00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.648
and it's cheap because it reads
answers agencies already gave.

179
00:11:09.648 --> 00:11:12.322
Load one vintage and
record what's missing.

180
00:11:12.322 --> 00:11:15.809
Detect gaps, from the answers
and then the directory.

181
00:11:15.809 --> 00:11:17.823
Put agentic systems first.

182
00:11:17.823 --> 00:11:21.511
Report one row per finding,
routed to a named role.

183
00:11:21.511 --> 00:11:24.548
And close a finding only
on a later observation.

184
00:11:24.548 --> 00:11:25.859
The scale is real.

185
00:11:25.859 --> 00:11:33.332
Of the 1,480 use cases recorded as piloted
or deployed in the published 2025 file,

186
00:11:33.332 --> 00:11:38.653
799 give no authorization
answer and 488 have no contact;

187
00:11:38.653 --> 00:11:41.823
72 percent trip at least one check.

188
00:11:41.823 --> 00:11:45.911
These are answers to reconcile,
not findings against agencies.

189
00:11:45.911 --> 00:11:51.110
The checks that matter most need the
directory, so only the agency can run them:

190
00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:56.310
no identity record, no accountable sponsor,
an identity with no inventory row,

191
00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.210
and a retired use case whose
identities still hold access.

192
00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:03.933
A central scan of the public file
could start that conversation,

193
00:12:03.933 --> 00:12:05.900
but it can't see a credential.

194
00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:09.425
Everything ends in the
authorization package.

195
00:12:09.425 --> 00:12:14.546
It has to answer five questions about each
AI system's identity: who owns it, how

196
00:12:14.546 --> 00:12:19.587
it authenticates, what it can reach, who's
watching it, and when its access ends.

197
00:12:19.587 --> 00:12:22.338
Each has an artifact
and controls behind it.

198
00:12:22.338 --> 00:12:23.688
Two points stand out.

199
00:12:23.688 --> 00:12:28.446
First, service identification and
authentication, control I A nine,

200
00:12:28.446 --> 00:12:32.611
describes an AI system calling
a tool with its own identity,

201
00:12:32.611 --> 00:12:35.925
and it's in no NIST baseline
and no FedRAMP class.

202
00:12:35.925 --> 00:12:37.700
So the program tailors it in.

203
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:42.414
Second, three of the seven high-impact
practices can be evidenced by the identity

204
00:12:42.414 --> 00:12:46.143
program: monitoring from the
system's own logs, training checked

205
00:12:46.143 --> 00:12:50.012
against the operators' group, and a
fail-safe that's a tested disable.

206
00:12:50.012 --> 00:12:55.276
The package can be machine-readable in OSCAL,
with no AI-specific structures needed.

207
00:12:55.276 --> 00:12:57.373
NIST's overlays for AI systems,

208
00:12:57.373 --> 00:13:01.738
renamed under the new executive
order, have no public draft yet.

209
00:13:01.738 --> 00:13:05.560
So tailor the baseline now,
and compare when they're final.

210
00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:07.685
So, four decisions.

211
00:13:07.685 --> 00:13:10.647
Count AI identities, not AI projects.

212
00:13:10.647 --> 00:13:14.762
No use case is recorded as
piloted or deployed without a link

213
00:13:14.762 --> 00:13:19.123
to its identity record, created
before any credential is issued.

214
00:13:19.123 --> 00:13:23.384
Put a person's name on every one:
an agency employee as sponsor,

215
00:13:23.384 --> 00:13:27.048
with HR's mover and leaver
events reaching the agent.

216
00:13:27.048 --> 00:13:31.914
Take away stored secrets and standing
privilege: federated credentials first,

217
00:13:31.914 --> 00:13:35.453
no standing privileged roles,
and a block-unless-approved

218
00:13:35.453 --> 00:13:39.435
policy kept in report-only mode
while agent targeting is preview.

219
00:13:39.435 --> 00:13:43.082
And authorize AI through
the process you already run:

220
00:13:43.082 --> 00:13:47.060
two signatures for high-impact
AI, with I A nine tailored in.

221
00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.210
The next step needs no new tooling.

222
00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:52.560
Run the inventory checks on
the agency's own submission,

223
00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.982
start with agentic systems
and missing authorizations,

224
00:13:55.982 --> 00:14:00.497
and confirm each agent capability for
your own cloud before planning on it.

225
00:14:00.497 --> 00:14:03.947
Every AI system needs an
identity and an owner.

226
00:14:03.947 --> 00:14:09.773
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
17 has the detail, every claim footnoted.

227
00:14:09.773 --> 00:14:10.600
Thank you.
